What is Iterable?

Iterable is an interface in Java.

It is used to iterate (loop) through a collection of objects one by one.

Without Iterable, you cannot use the for-each loop.

Syntax
public interface Iterable<T>

Example

Imagine you have a basket of fruits.

Apple

Orange

Mango

You want to take each fruit one by one.

Instead of saying

"Give me fruit 1, fruit 2, fruit 3..."

You simply say

"Give me each fruit."

That's exactly what Iterable does.
Why use Iterable?
To traverse elements one by one.
Supports enhanced for loop (for-each).
Provides an Iterator object.

Example 1 - Using for-each loop
import java.util.*;

public class Main
{
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        ArrayList<String> list = new ArrayList<>();

        list.add("Apple");
        list.add("Orange");
        list.add("Mango");

        for(String fruit : list)
        {
            System.out.println(fruit);
        }
    }
}
Output
Apple
Orange
Mango
Java internally does this:

Iterator<String> it = list.iterator();

while(it.hasNext())
{
    String fruit = it.next();
    System.out.println(fruit);
}

So for-each uses Iterator internally.

Example 2 - Using Iterator manually
import java.util.*;

public class Main
{
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        ArrayList<String> list = new ArrayList<>();

        list.add("Apple");
        list.add("Orange");
        list.add("Mango");

        Iterator<String> it = list.iterator();

        while(it.hasNext())
        {
            System.out.println(it.next());
        }
    }
}

Output

Apple
Orange
Mango


What is List?

List is an interface in the Java Collection Framework.

It is used to store multiple objects in an ordered manner.

It also allows duplicate values.

Syntax
List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();
Why do we use List?

We use a List when we need to:

 Store multiple values.
 Maintain insertion order.
Allow duplicate values.
Access elements using an index.
Example
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        List<String> fruits = new ArrayList<>();

        fruits.add("Apple");
        fruits.add("Orange");
        fruits.add("Apple");

        System.out.println(fruits);
    }
}
Output
[Apple, Orange, Apple]

List methods
Add()=>Add an element
Add(index,element)=>Adds an element at a specific index.
get(index)=>Returns the element at the given index.
set(index, element)=>Replaces an element.
remove(index)=>Removes an element using its index.
remove(Object)=>Removes the first matching object.
size()=>Returns the number of elements.
contains()=>Checks whether an element exists.
isEmpty()=>Checks whether the list is empty.
clear()=>Removes all element
indexOf()=>Returns the first occurrence index.
 Example
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();

        list.add("Apple");
        list.add("Orange");
        list.add("Mango");

        System.out.println("List : " + list);

        System.out.println("Size : " + list.size());

        System.out.println("Get : " + list.get(1));

        list.set(1, "Banana");

        System.out.println("After Set : " + list);

        list.remove("Apple");

        System.out.println("After Remove : " + list);

        System.out.println("Contains Mango : " + list.contains("Mango"));

        System.out.println("Index : " + list.indexOf("Mango"));

        System.out.println("Is Empty : " + list.isEmpty());

        list.clear();

        System.out.println("After Clear : " + list);
    }
}
output
List : [Apple, Orange, Mango]
Size : 3
Get : Orange
After Set : [Apple, Banana, Mango]
After Remove : [Banana, Mango]
Contains Mango : true
Index : 1
Is Empty : false
After Clear : []

